meta {
  name: Test Desktop
  type: http
  seq: 2
}

get {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/api/notifications/test-desktop?alertType=orb-up&ticker=AAPL&direction=UP&timeframe=M5
  body: none
  auth: none
}

params:query {
  alertType: orb-up
  ticker: AAPL
  direction: UP
  timeframe: M5
}

headers {
  Accept: application/json
}

docs {
  # Notifications — Test Desktop (macOS banner)

  Fires a TEST native macOS desktop banner on demand so you can verify the
  desktop-notification feature without waiting for a live alert.

  Banner body is the format used for real alerts:
  `<timeframe> <TICKER> <emoji> <BUY|SELL> -- <type>` — UP → 🟢 BUY, DOWN → 🔴 SELL.
  e.g. `M5 AAPL 🟢 BUY -- ORB`
  The alert id is normalised to a short label: orb-up/orb-down → ORB,
  swift-trend → Swift, st-adx → ST+ADX.

  ## Query (all optional)
  - `alertType` — raw alert id (default "orb-up"), e.g. "orb-up", "swift-trend", "st-adx".
  - `ticker`    — symbol (default "AAPL").
  - `direction` — UP or DOWN (default "UP").
  - `timeframe` — e.g. M5, M3 (default "M5").

  ## Response (200)
  { desktopEnabled, sent, preview?, note/hint, timestamp }
  - `desktopEnabled=false` → the feature is off or you're not on macOS. Set
    `app.notifications.desktop-enabled: true` and run on the Mac GUI session.
  - `sent=true` → a banner was fired on the local Mac. If nothing appeared, check
    macOS System Settings → Notifications permissions for your terminal/JVM.

  ## Notes
  - Desktop banners only work when the app runs on the Mac you're sitting at
    (local GUI session) — not headless/server. Telegram is unaffected either way.
  - Fail-safe: a notification error never disrupts scanning/trading.
}
